Model training and validation were performed offline on the Sapelo2 GPU environment operated by the Georgia Advanced Computing Resource Center at the University of Georgia [cite:gacrc_sapelo2]. The Jetson boards were used for deployment benchmarking and energy measurement, not for training. The training workflow followed the same high-level structure for all model families:

 prepare locked dataset splits and runtime manifests;
 run a smoke-training check where applicable;
 perform hyperparameter tuning where applicable;
 train the full model;
 validate on the validation split;
 evaluate on the held-out test split;
 export the accepted model to ONNX;
 archive metrics, manifests, checksums, and selected qualitative outputs.

The benchmark retained seven trained model families spanning lightweight detection, larger detection, lightweight segmentation, conventional CNN segmentation, and transformer-style segmentation:

 YOLO11n and YOLO11s for YOLO-family object detection;
 RT-DETR-R18 for non-YOLO detection;
 BiSeNetV2 and MobileNetV3-LRASPP for lightweight semantic segmentation;
 DeepLabV3+ for heavier CNN-based segmentation;
 SegFormer-B0 for transformer-style semantic segmentation.

Two NVIDIA Jetson development kits were used:

 NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin 64GB Developer Kit;
 NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano Super Developer Kit.

The primary benchmark boundary was pure TensorRT engine-level inference. The timed region excluded:

 JPEG/image loading and dataset file I/O;
 CPU-side preprocessing and normalization;
 postprocessing and non-maximum suppression;
 mask decoding outside the engine;
 visualization and prediction saving;
 framework-specific per-image path overhead.

Input-resolution experiments were performed as a supporting ablation, not as a replacement for the locked 640 matrix. The ablation identified:

 ⟨S_ ⟩: the smallest accuracy-preserving input size;
 ⟨S_ ⟩: the largest memory-safe common native input size across both Jetson boards.

Long-duration battery continuation tests were used to evaluate sustained deployment behaviour under a field-like energy source. Each main battery run followed the same procedure:

 charge the UGREEN power bank to 100%;
 connect the board-specific power-source path through the external logger;
 boot the Jetson from the power bank;
 confirm the active nvpmodel mode;
 confirm that the external logger reports the expected board-input voltage and nonzero power;
 start internal telemetry logging;
 enter the noise-guard screening context where supported;
 start the pure-engine TensorRT benchmark;
 continue until the power-bank display reaches 20% remaining;
 stop the run cleanly using manual Ctrl-C;
 restore the noise-guard context after the run where applicable;
 save summaries, latency files, event records, noise-guard summaries, sanitized run-validity summaries, external logger archives, internal telemetry, and stop annotations.

The main continuation configurations were:

 YOLO11s INT8, 640, AGX 15 W and Nano Super 15 W;
 SegFormer-B0 INT8, 640, AGX 15 W and Nano Super 15 W;
 SegFormer-B0 FP16, 640, AGX 50 W and Nano Super 25 W.

A benchmark run was considered valid only if all required configuration and measurement conditions were satisfied:

 the intended board and nvpmodel mode were confirmed;
 the intended model, precision, input size, and batch size were used;
 the timing boundary matched the planned pure-engine or supplementary boundary;
 the external logger was active and external energy was integrable;

external voltage was recorded throughout each run and checked for consistency with the board-compatible upstream source path described in Section [ref:sec:external_power]; no main battery-continuation run was excluded for a voltage-validity failure;
 logger gaps greater than 1 s were counted and reported; the six main battery-continuation runs had zero such gaps; 
 noise-guard summaries did not report fatal guard errors where the guard was used;
 run-validity summaries and diagnostic status records did not indicate benchmark failure;
 failed inference count was zero;
 no unexpected reboot, OOM event, or overtemperature shutdown occurred;
 battery tests used the standardized 100% to 20% stop rule;
 alarm events were saved and reported transparently.

Four short idle baseline runs were collected to estimate board/mode baseline external power ⟨P_0⟩:

 AGX 15 W idle;
 AGX 50 W idle;
 Nano Super 15 W idle;
 Nano Super 25 W idle.

All evidence was archived in AgriJetsonBench_v1. The package includes:

 dataset split files, class names, and license notes;
 training summaries, validation/test metrics, training reproducibility summaries, and artifact manifests;
 model complexity summaries at 640;
 Jetson hardware/software version outputs;
 TensorRT engine metadata and model artifact manifests;
 battery run summaries, latency CSV files, event records, noise-guard summaries, sanitized run-validity summaries, and external logger archives;
 internal telemetry logs and time-aligned internal--external agreement tables;
 idle baseline summaries;
 energy-model validation tables;
 paper figures, supplementary tables, scripts, and manifest files.
